 "money": -1.62}, "rating_margin": -12.5, "sigma_race": 6.0, "sigma_total": 6.78, "baseline_margin": -8.87, "analyst_adjustment": -1.63, "key_factors": ["R+8 district lean with incumbent Beth Van Duyne running", "Expert ratings cluster Safe/Likely R (consensus implied margin ~R+12)", "Huge financial edge: Van Duyne $2.9M cash vs. Burge $37K", "No general-election polling available"], "rationale": "The fundamentals point to a comfortable Republican hold: an R+8 seat, a well-funded incumbent with no outside opposition spending, and a Democratic challenger who has raised little. Expert raters mostly call it Safe or Likely R and markets price Democratic chances near 7%, so the forecast sits slightly right of the fundamentals-only estimate but short of the most bullish ratings.", "watch": "A late surge of Democratic outside spending or polling showing Van Duyne under 55% would signal a tighter race.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Beth Van Duyne (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Beth Van Duyne seeks a fourth term in a North Texas district that leans Republican by about eight points. She enters with a large financial advantage, roughly $2.9 million on hand against Kevin Burge's $37,000, and no recorded outside spending against her. Burge emerged from a contested Democratic primary but has shown little fundraising traction.\n\nMost expert raters place the race in the Safe or Likely Republican column, and prediction markets give Democrats only a small chance. With no public general-election polling, the forecast rests on district lean, incumbency, and money, all of which favor the incumbent. A meaningful upset would require an unforeseen shift in the national environment or a late influx of Democratic resources.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0657, "margin": -9.69}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0832, "margin": -10.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.